1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And here's a problem that creeps up on every long session.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Your context window fills and nothing empties on its own.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Every turn piles on more history and more tool output

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
until the model starts to drift.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson covers what accumulates,

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
why it costs you, and how state survives a session.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Let's break it down.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Three terms anchor this whole lesson.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
So let's pin them down.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
First, the context window.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
That's the token budget the model can read

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
in a single request, and it holds the system prompt,

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
the running history, your tool definitions,

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
and every tool result, all competing for the same space.

15
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Second, durable state.

16
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
That's knowledge you keep outside the live window,

17
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
in a file or in config, so it survives a truncation

18
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
or the end of a session.

19
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Third, the session boundary.

20
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That's the line where one working session ends

21
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
and a fresh window begins, carrying nothing forward

22
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,000
unless you deliberately saved it.

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Window, durable state, boundary, hold onto those three.

24
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Let's look at what actually piles up while you work,

25
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
because it's more than you'd think.

26
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Within a single session, everything accumulates.

27
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The system prompt is present on every turn.

28
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Your tool definitions are present on every turn.

29
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The full back and forth of the conversation

30
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
keeps growing as you go.

31
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
And the real budget killer is large tool output,

32
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
a giant file you read, a huge search result.

33
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
They sit in the window, taking up space

34
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
long after they stop being useful.

35
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Here's the part people miss.

36
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Nothing gets removed automatically.

37
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
The model doesn't quietly forget

38
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
the boring stuff to make room.

39
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
If you want something out of that window,

40
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
your code has to take it out.

41
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Now let's separate two kinds of state,

42
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
because mixing them up causes real bugs.

43
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Ephemeral context is whatever sits

44
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
in the live window right now.

45
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
It's wonderfully fast.

46
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The model sees it instantly, but it's fragile.

47
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The moment the window truncates or the session ends, it's gone.

48
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Durable state is the opposite.

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
You write it outside the window to a file or to config,

50
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
and it survives.

51
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It survives compaction.

52
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
It survives a session boundary.

53
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
It's still there tomorrow.

54
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
So the rule writes itself.

55
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Anything you need to remember for the long haul,

56
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
a decision, a fact, a running record,

57
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
doesn't belong in the live window.

58
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
It belongs somewhere durable, on disk,

59
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
where a reset can't erase it.

60
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
So why care about all this accumulation?

61
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
A bloated window costs you in three concrete ways.

62
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The first is higher cost.

63
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Every token in the window gets reprocessed

64
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
on every single turn.

65
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
So a huge window means a bigger bill,

66
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
whether or not the extra content is helping.

67
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The second is slower responses.

68
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
More tokens to read on each call adds latency.

69
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So replies take longer as the window grows.

70
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The third is drift and errors.

71
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
As low signal history piles up,

72
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
the model follows your instructions less reliably.

73
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
And the context that actually matters

74
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,000
gets crowded out by noise.

75
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
So this isn't housekeeping for its own sake.

76
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Three real costs are exactly why session hygiene

77
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,000
earns its place.

78
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Let's get practical about the single biggest offender,

79
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,000
old tool output.

80
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
When a tool dumps a giant result, a whole file,

81
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
a long search listing,

82
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
that result doesn't just cost you once.

83
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
It sits in the window and gets reprocessed

84
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
on every later turn,

85
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
quietly draining your budget the whole time.

86
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Pruning is the fix.

87
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
You drop that one stale result

88
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
without touching anything else in the conversation.

89
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
You're not nuking the session.

90
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
You're just clearing the noise

91
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
that's already served its purpose.

92
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Do this and the high signal content, your actual task,

93
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
your recent decisions,

94
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
stays right where the model can see it.

95
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Think of it as clearing your desk,

96
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,000
not burning the room down.

97
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
So what actually carries knowledge

98
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
from one session to the next?

99
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Three facts settle it.

100
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
First, every Claude code session

101
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
starts with a fresh context window.

102
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
The model remembers nothing from last time on its own.

103
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Second, Claude.md files carry instructions you write.

104
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
They load at the start of every session

105
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and the project root file re-injects

106
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
even after a compaction.

107
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So your durable rules always persist.

108
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Third, auto memory carries learnings Claude writes for itself.

109
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
It lives on disk under a memory.md index

110
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
and reloads as needed.

111
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,000
So the split is simple.

112
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
You author Claude.md, Claude maintains its own memory

113
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
and the fresh window gets re-grounded from both.

114
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Now, if you're building on the API directly

115
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
rather than inside Claude code,

116
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
the session boundary is entirely yours to draw.

117
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Remember, the API is stateless.

118
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
It keeps no memory between calls.

119
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
So all of your conversation state

120
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
lives in the request you send.

121
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Your code owns the transcript

122
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
and you resend it on every single request.

123
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
That has a useful consequence.

124
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Starting a new session

125
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
just means resetting that message history,

126
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
dropping the old turns and beginning a fresh array.

127
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
You get to decide exactly what carries over,

128
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
maybe a short summary, maybe nothing.

129
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And then you re-ground the new session

130
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
from your config and system prompt.

131
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:33,000
The boundary isn't magic.

132
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
It's a choice you make in code.

133
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Let's name what goes wrong when you skip all this

134
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
because the failure is quiet.

135
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Left unmanaged, a long session degrades

136
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
in ways that sneak up on you.

137
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Instruction following weakens first.

138
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
As low signal tokens crowd the window,

139
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,000
the model has a harder time

140
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
keeping your actual rules in focus.

141
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Then early context gets pushed out

142
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
of the model's effective attention.

143
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So things you established up front start slipping

144
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
and your costs climb the whole time,

145
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
even when the useful content hasn't grown at all

146
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
because you're paying to reprocess noise.

147
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Good hygiene is what keeps a long run

148
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
as sharp at the end as it was at the very start.

149
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
You stay deliberate about what's in the window.

150
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Here's the bottom line.

151
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Managing session state is active engineering,

152
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
not something the model does for you.

153
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
The window only accumulates,

154
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
so nothing leaves unless you take it out.

155
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Just remember, ephemeral context vanishes

156
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
while durable state lives on disk.

157
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,000
That covers managing context and session state.

158
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Take a breath, and I'll see you in the next lesson.

